//? Title: Lagged Fibonacci through actual ring updates
//? Subtitle: Modular operands, original reproducibility and exact finite parity cycles
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A pseudorandom engine must generate a repeatable stream while retaining a
compact internal state. An additive lagged-Fibonacci engine updates one word of
a lagged ring using two earlier words modulo2^64. We inspect actual operands and
ring positions, preserve the original five reproducibility comparisons, and
expose the low-bit linear recurrence through exact finite parity-state cycles.
Reproducibility and small projected periods are distinct from statistical
quality, cryptographic security and the full64-bit engine period.
)

\toc

> The actual ring recurrence

With0<j<k, keep k unsigned64 words ordered by age. The oldest word represents
x[n−k], and the word j places behind the next output represents x[n−j]. The
update is

$$ x_n=(x_{n-j}+x_{n-k})\bmod 2^{64}. $$
\alttext(The next word is the sum of the two lagged words with intended unsigned64 wraparound.)

The physical index points to the oldest slot. The short operand is index−j
wrapped within k, and the sum overwrites the oldest slot before the index
advances. SplitMix64(seed) supplies the initial k words in physical order; this
seeding convention is part of the native stream. The shared next_step and
next_u64 use exactly the same single transition. Raw inspection does not reset
the inherited distribution cache.

Construction and storage cost O(k); each raw update takes O(1) fixed-width work.
Copying an engine copies both ring position and distribution cache. The
arithmetic recurrence is not a cryptographic generator, arbitrary lag choices do
not imply maximal period, and the scalar seed selects at most2^64 initial
states.

> See actual operands before wraparound

Use the original seed2026 and lags j2,k5. The trace preallocates all
observations before consuming eight real transitions, retaining complete
before/after boundary rings. The diagram shows the actual first update’s parity
projection; the table retains its full64-bit operands and outputs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lagged_fibonacci_update.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/lagged_fibonacci_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::random::LaggedFibonacci64 g(2026, 2, 5);
    const auto t = dm::random::lagged_fibonacci_trace(g, 8);
    std::print("| actual update | overwrite slot | short slot | long operand | short operand | modular " "output | next slot |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.steps.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &e = t.steps[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, e.write_index,
                   e.short_index, e.long_word, e.short_word, e.value,
                   e.next_index);
    }
    const auto &e = t.steps.front();
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("long", {0, 1},
           std::format("Actual oldest slot {}\nlow bit {}", e.write_index,
                       e.long_word & 1ULL)).node("short", {0, -1},
                                                 std::format(
                                                 "Actual short slot {}\nlow bit {}",
                                                 e.short_index,
                                                 e.short_word & 1ULL)).node(
        "sum", {6, 0},
        std::format("Actual modular output\nlow bit {}", e.value & 1ULL)).node(
        "next", {12, 0}, std::format("Actual next slot\n{}", e.next_index)).edge("long", "sum").edge("short", "sum").edge("sum", "next").save_svg("build/lagged_fibonacci_update.svg");
}
```
