> Original five reproducibility comparisons

The original example constructed two engines with seed2026,j2,k5 and compared
five successive raw outputs. Retain exactly that experiment, now exposing the
actual shared transition outputs rather than five unexplained true flags. The
plot deliberately shows only each output’s low16 bits; the table is the
complete64-bit evidence. Equal streams from equal initial state establish
deterministic reproducibility, not randomness or independence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lagged_fibonacci_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/lagged_fibonacci_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::random::LaggedFibonacci64 a(2026, 2, 5), b(2026, 2, 5);
    const auto t = dm::random::lagged_fibonacci_trace(a, 5);
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| original comparison | actual first stream word | actual second stream word | equal |\n| " "---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        const auto v = b.next_u64();
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, t.steps[i].value, v,
                   t.steps[i].value == v);
        x.push_back(i);
        y.push_back(t.steps[i].value & 65535ULL);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "low16 bits", {37, 99, 235}, 6).y_limits(-2000, 72000).title(
        "Original five outputs: explicit low16-bit view").x_label(
        "actual output ordinal").y_label("output modulo65536").save_svg(
        "build/lagged_fibonacci_original.svg");
}
```
